1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,197
An AWS compliance finding tells us something may be wrong.

2
00:00:05,357 --> 00:00:14,030
It does not tell us who may change it, which exact change is approved, or whether the result has been independently verified.

3
00:00:14,190 --> 00:00:19,947
AWSOps, the backend of the Defence Factory prototype, is built around that gap.

4
00:00:20,107 --> 00:00:25,524
The interesting part is not a model generating an AWS command.

5
00:00:25,685 --> 00:00:34,520
It is the boundary between evidence, an exact proposed action, a genuine human decision, and an outcome we can defend.

6
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:36,920
A finding is not authority.

7
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:39,491
Approval is not execution.

8
00:00:39,651 --> 00:00:43,778
A successful operation is not fresh compliance evidence.

9
00:00:43,938 --> 00:00:47,330
We will follow one disposable S3 canary.

10
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:55,926
This walkthrough is synthetic and grounded in repository code, not a recording of a successful AWS change.

11
00:00:56,086 --> 00:01:05,186
Keep four questions separate: what ran, what the provider observed, what compliance evaluated, and how fresh that evaluation is.

12
00:01:05,346 --> 00:01:07,735
Missing answers should stay unknown.

13
00:01:08,345 --> 00:01:16,764
The shared contract distinguishes findings, controls, target registrations, batches, decisions, runs, and evidence.

14
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:19,194
These records make different claims.

15
00:01:19,353 --> 00:01:23,296
An envelope has a schema version, a kind, and a record.

16
00:01:23,456 --> 00:01:25,653
Unknown fields are rejected.

17
00:01:25,813 --> 00:01:35,284
The bounded synthetic intake limits bytes and rows, rejects duplicate keys and conflicting identities, and keeps imported content inert.

18
00:01:35,444 --> 00:01:38,345
Its supported accounts are mock aliases.

19
00:01:38,505 --> 00:01:44,763
Our public resource alias represents a disposable canary, never a private bucket name.

20
00:01:44,923 --> 00:01:49,510
Even a structurally valid finding remains an imported assertion.

21
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:53,105
It cannot attest an AWS observation.

22
00:01:53,265 --> 00:01:59,129
A target registration is a public projection, not an IAM trust relationship.

23
00:01:59,289 --> 00:02:04,630
A private backend must separately resolve the approved identity and resource.

24
00:02:04,789 --> 00:02:13,109
The companion Agent Core Two interface consumes evidence; its presence does not prove a live integrated deployment.

25
00:02:13,269 --> 00:02:17,208
The offline planner and the canary adapter are separate paths.

26
00:02:17,368 --> 00:02:28,078
The same public alias connects our explanation, but imported evidence does not become permission or a trusted provider read by moving across the diagram.

27
00:02:28,688 --> 00:02:33,107
Approval is meaningful only when it names an exact operation.

28
00:02:33,267 --> 00:02:39,560
Here we enter the canary adapter's separate preparation path, using a synthetic observation.

29
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:48,291
The batch binds one target, its revision and evidence digest, the registry version, mode, frozen time, and expiry.

30
00:02:48,452 --> 00:02:55,172
Its capability is require TLS; its expected change is deny insecure transport.

31
00:02:55,332 --> 00:03:00,143
The scope digest covers the complete batch with that digest field omitted.

32
00:03:00,302 --> 00:03:05,143
Canonical object keys are sorted; target array order is retained.

33
00:03:05,304 --> 00:03:10,209
Change the target or evidence revision and the seal no longer matches.

34
00:03:10,369 --> 00:03:15,958
The adapter also checks the private binding and current evidence before execution.

35
00:03:16,118 --> 00:03:23,179
Expired preparation or changed provider state cannot silently inherit the earlier approval.

36
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:30,636
The canary reuses a maximum five minute preparation window and a sixty second observation age check.

37
00:03:30,796 --> 00:03:34,359
These limits do not turn a hash into authentication.

38
00:03:34,518 --> 00:03:40,042
The older single finding Frozen Scope is a different type, not this batch schema.

39
00:03:40,202 --> 00:03:48,926
The application additionally binds the principal, source revision, fixed setup actions, cost bound, and any conditional cleanup.

40
00:03:49,536 --> 00:03:53,503
The decision must come from a trusted authenticated backend.

41
00:03:53,663 --> 00:04:02,628
Possessing the digest, typing a phrase, or importing a document that says approve does not identify an authorized person.

42
00:04:02,788 --> 00:04:13,383
Inside the canary adapter, an immediate database transaction selects the saved batch and checks the principal, binding, scope, mode, and expiry.

43
00:04:13,543 --> 00:04:19,410
It commits the winning decision and initial run together, before any provider operation.

44
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:23,026
Approve Once and Reject both consume that slot.

45
00:04:23,186 --> 00:04:26,215
A competing choice cannot replace the winner.

46
00:04:26,375 --> 00:04:31,087
In this illustrative race, Reject wins: zero provider writes.

47
00:04:31,247 --> 00:04:41,800
The application also claims a verified approval packet once; replay of the same packet reopens the same result instead of creating another run.

48
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:50,551
Now the important current boundary: the legacy native store records rejected or approve blocked, with dispatch disabled.

49
00:04:50,711 --> 00:05:00,528
The ordinary live canary operator refuses approval before constructing a provider because its trusted human approval binding is missing.

50
00:05:00,687 --> 00:05:05,104
The injected test verifier authorizes only a mock provider.

51
00:05:05,264 --> 00:05:12,063
Our approved branch is a synthetic explanation of the implementation, not a real owner's decision.

52
00:05:12,673 --> 00:05:20,360
The canary is deliberately narrow: a new, empty, task owned lab bucket and a fixed TLS only policy.

53
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:29,893
The provider checks the selected personal lab profile and Region, then compares a fresh non root identity with the private expected account.

54
00:05:30,053 --> 00:05:33,125
A preexisting bucket is not eligible.

55
00:05:33,285 --> 00:05:43,966
The fixed setup sequence creates the bucket, writes task tags, enables all four public access block flags, and applies the reviewed transport policy.

56
00:05:44,126 --> 00:05:50,121
Public access blocking and denying non TLS transport are different protections.

57
00:05:50,281 --> 00:05:53,460
This is not encryption at rest remediation.

58
00:05:53,619 --> 00:05:58,372
The policy denies S3 actions when secure transport is false.

59
00:05:58,533 --> 00:06:03,918
There is no model accessible arbitrary AWS method in this path.

60
00:06:04,077 --> 00:06:18,528
Before the policy write, fresh reads require acknowledged task creation, exact tags, an empty object listing, no versions or delete markers, the expected public block, and no existing policy.

61
00:06:18,688 --> 00:06:21,888
Each mutation has a durable ledger row.

62
00:06:22,048 --> 00:06:29,419
Intent becomes calling immediately before the SDK boundary, and an acknowledged call becomes ACK.

63
00:06:29,579 --> 00:06:34,843
A merged IAM proposal alone does not prove its role is installed.

64
00:06:35,453 --> 00:06:41,630
Suppose the policy request crosses the SDK boundary, but its response disappears.

65
00:06:41,790 --> 00:06:45,630
That is not proof AWS rejected the request.

66
00:06:45,790 --> 00:06:49,875
The operation stays calling and execution stays unknown.

67
00:06:50,035 --> 00:06:54,871
Automatically replaying it would turn uncertainty into another mutation.

68
00:06:55,031 --> 00:07:03,886
The provider disables automatic mutation retries, and reconciliation reads the same run instead of minting a fresh batch.

69
00:07:04,046 --> 00:07:13,099
If every setup row is acknowledged, reconciliation can recover execution succeeded after a crash before the final result save.

70
00:07:13,259 --> 00:07:15,636
A calling row remains uncertain.

71
00:07:15,795 --> 00:07:23,821
The exported write count counts conservative SDK mutation attempts, not independently proven cloud changes.

72
00:07:23,981 --> 00:07:27,778
A known pre call refusal adds no attempted write.

73
00:07:27,939 --> 00:07:34,644
In our mock example, those attempts belong in simulated writes and real writes remains zero.

74
00:07:34,803 --> 00:07:37,107
Cleanup is not an escape hatch.

75
00:07:37,267 --> 00:07:46,303
One authenticated packet may cover both setup and exact conditional cleanup, but omitting cleanup grants no deletion authority.

76
00:07:46,463 --> 00:07:51,832
Fresh ownership, emptiness, operation state, and cleanup expiry still matter.

77
00:07:51,993 --> 00:07:59,915
An uncertain operation blocks destructive cleanup; a task tag or time to live does not schedule deletion.

78
00:08:00,525 --> 00:08:06,616
Even when the SDK sequence succeeds, the result is not automatically verified.

79
00:08:06,775 --> 00:08:12,606
The adapter reads provider state independently and checks the intended transport policy.

80
00:08:12,767 --> 00:08:16,740
A matching policy proves only what that readback observed.

81
00:08:16,899 --> 00:08:22,019
It does not trigger or prove a fresh AWS Config evaluation.

82
00:08:22,179 --> 00:08:33,186
Our synthetic successful branch therefore has execution succeeded and provider readback match, while compliance stays unknown and freshness stays not verified.

83
00:08:33,346 --> 00:08:35,362
The run remains needs review.

84
00:08:35,522 --> 00:08:40,237
These are real schema values, not four names for the same green light.

85
00:08:40,397 --> 00:08:52,052
The shared contract requires the selected targets and independent evidence before verified can be justified; this adapter deliberately does not make that claim.

86
00:08:52,212 --> 00:08:57,597
The evidence also separates the source revision from an observed hosted version.

87
00:08:57,756 --> 00:08:59,932
Here hosted version is null.

88
00:09:00,092 --> 00:09:04,977
The schema's mock version suffix alone is not a deployment receipt.

89
00:09:05,138 --> 00:09:18,462
A private durable ledger preserves recovery information, but a privileged host writer is still a trust assumption; a digest or local hash chain is not an independent tamper proof signature.

90
00:09:19,072 --> 00:09:34,066
The repository contains concrete engineering: bounded intake, shared contracts, exact scope binding, durable decisions, a fixed provider, uncertainty handling, and evidence that refuses to overclaim.

91
00:09:34,226 --> 00:09:44,003
The source tests assert approval, rejection, drift, expiry, replay exclusion, and uncertain acknowledgement behavior with fake providers.

92
00:09:44,163 --> 00:09:47,960
That is synthetic proof, not a live owner approval.

93
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:54,328
There is also accepted historical AWS Config read proof from October eleventh.

94
00:09:54,488 --> 00:10:01,571
It establishes bounded account local acquisition, not evaluation freshness or remediation.

95
00:10:01,731 --> 00:10:15,554
The later transfer record still retains ledger provenance and initialization, exact deployed pins, genuine normal login Reject, and provider and compliance verification as pending outcomes.

96
00:10:15,714 --> 00:10:21,525
The old execution issue closed as transferred, not completed product acceptance.

97
00:10:21,685 --> 00:10:27,805
We checked the source at the reviewed revision; this film performed no AWS calls.

98
00:10:27,965 --> 00:10:32,268
The proposition is controlled change with inspectable evidence.

99
00:10:32,428 --> 00:10:41,754
The next useful test is to close the exact remaining live acceptance gap, not add another model or turn missing evidence green.

100
00:10:41,914 --> 00:10:47,791
What was approved, what ran, and what the evidence proves must remain separate questions.
